# Benchmarks and measurements

Correctness comes first. A performance claim comes with a measurement in the same PR.

## Tools

- Micro-benchmarks: BenchmarkDotNet with `[MemoryDiagnoser]` in `benchmarks/AuraORM.Benchmarks` (`benchmarks/README.md`). Comparisons: raw ADO.NET (the floor), Dapper, Dapper.AOT (source-generated reading, as AuraORM's), linq2db (a typed query builder, as AuraORM), EF Core (compiled and non-compiled queries), and for SQL building the SQL builders SqExpress, SqlKata, and Sqlify (what a user who looks for a SQL builder compares), on the same database, data, and machine. Not a gate; CI builds the project and does not run it on shared runners.
- Before a benchmark is measured, `--check` shows that every compared library returns the same rows: a comparison of different work is not a comparison.
- Profiles: Intel VTune (`C:\Program Files (x86)\Intel\oneAPItune\latestin64tune.exe`), `-collect hotspots -knob sampling-mode=hw`, on a console loop that runs one path for 10 to 20 seconds. Hardware sampling needs administrator rights: run the script elevated through UAC (`Start-Process pwsh -Verb RunAs -Wait -ArgumentList ...`, output to files), never fall back to user-mode sampling. Runtime functions get names with `-finalize -search-dir srv*<cache>*https://msdl.microsoft.com/download/symbols`; the instructions of one function come from `-report hotspots -source-object function=<name> -group-by address`.
- Allocation tests that are gates (`docs/design.ja.md` names them) measure `GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread()` after a warm-up. An allowed count stays or goes down; raising it needs an Issue.

## Rules

- Performance acceptance is judged on a Release build; NativeAOT figures are reported beside JIT figures when the claim is about AOT.
- Profile before a speed row, and write on the Issue where the time goes.
- Compare both sides with the same configuration, data, and machine state, interleaved (A, B, B, A), three rounds or more. A difference inside the spread is not a result.
- A benchmark against a database separates the library's cost from the database's: measure SQL rendering and row materialization on their own (an in-memory reader), and the whole round trip on SQLite in memory.
- Fix the counts of a run (`--launchCount 3 --warmupCount 5 --iterationCount 15 --iterationTime 250`) and write them with the results. BenchmarkDotNet's default job adds iterations, up to 100, while a measurement is noisy: on #95 its estimate for the whole suite was 17 hours, and the fixed counts took 35 minutes.
- Allocations are measured with BenchmarkDotNet's `[MemoryDiagnoser]`. A measurement of its own (`GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread`) calls the code until the JIT has optimized it with dynamic PGO (on the order of a million calls) and says so: the same statement allocated 3,024 B in its first thousand calls, 3,000 B without dynamic PGO, and 2,864 B after the optimization, which is what BenchmarkDotNet reported (#99, found by the review).
- A breakdown is the difference between chains that each add one step, measured the same way, and it adds up to the whole. Steps measured apart added up to 2,952 B for a whole of 3,024 B (#99).
- A profile counts the time of an inlined call in its caller, so a profile alone does not choose a change: a prototype measures it. #94 read most of the gap of row reading as two async state machines per row; a prototype that removed one of them took a tenth.
- When two prototypes leave a breakdown open, the next step is the profile, read at the instruction level, not a third prototype. On #106 an hour of probe benchmarks came before the profile, and the owner had to ask for it.
- Dynamic PGO inlines the row factory, the generated `ReadRow`, and the `RowReader` methods into the loop of `Executor.ListAsync` (guarded devirtualization of the delegate). A profile of row reading shows one function; the split is in its instructions (#106).
- A per-row figure from 100 rows minus 1 row can hold a cost that is not per row. Before a gap is put on the rows, measure 1,000 rows minus 1 row too: on #106 a gap of 4.5 ns per row at 100 rows was 1.1 ns at 1,000.
- A median is read from BenchmarkDotNet's JSON (`--exporters json`, `Statistics.Median`). The Median column of its table appears only for some distributions; a script that read the table stopped on #103.
- A call shorter than the clock's resolution is repeated inside one sample until the sample is long enough.
- A figure that moved is explained from the data, not from a guess (`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc`).
- A target names what it measures before it gets a number.
- A measurement exercises the cost that the change adds. A change that adds stage types for two to eight tables is measured with statements that join two to eight tables; a single-table statement uses none of the new types and shows no difference whatever they cost. When the code before the change cannot express the measured case, say so and compare the new cases with each other.
- A gain inside the spread is reverted, not shipped with other changes. A change tried for speed and not kept is reported with its measurement, so that it is not tried again.
- A design that names a technique for its speed is measured before the design PR says so. A prototype in a scratch project is enough.
- Before a comparison, check each compared library's latest stable release on NuGet and use it; the results name the versions.
